---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";

const postings = [
  ["1832", "New South Wales", "Arrives with the 4th (King's Own) Regiment"],
  ["1837", "Camden", "Marries Catherine Margaret Mulcahy, 13 February"],
  ["1837", "Kempsey", "William Hartley born 30 September — the direct ancestor"],
  ["1839", "Vale of Clwydd", "Sergeant of Mounted Police. The Chubb capture, 30 October"],
  ["1840", "Kempsey", "James Goulbourn born"],
  ["1842", "Braidwood", "John Braidwood born — named for the posting"],
  ["1844", "Sydney", "Ellen Mary born"],
  ["1846–47", "Goulburn", "Joseph Samuel and Emma Martha born"],
  ["1851–53", "New South Wales", "Samuel Hartley Hanley Stafford, and Elizabeth Margaret"],
  ["1849", "Moreton Bay", "Chief constable — the head of policing in Brisbane"],
  ["1855", "Brisbane", "Arthur Oliver born — the family has moved north"],
  ["1859", "Brisbane", "Retires as chief constable; promoted Governor of the gaol. Marries Margaret Hyland, 24 November"],
  ["1885", "Enoggera", "Dies suddenly, 4 July, aged 75. Buried Toowong, grave 13-17-1"],
];
---
<Base
  title="Sergeant Sneyd"
  eyebrow="Samuel Charles Sneyd, 1810–1885"
  dek="A Hanley grocer's son who took the King's shilling, sailed for New South Wales in 1832, shot a bushranger dead in the Blue Mountains in 1839, and finished as Governor of Brisbane Gaol. He is the best-documented man in this archive, and until now nobody had written him down."

>
  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">When the archive stripped the Sneyds of their invented descent from Keele Hall, it
    was braced to lose something. It gained this instead: not a squire but a
    <strong>sergeant of Mounted Police</strong>, and a life that can be traced through the birthplaces
    of his own children. <a href={u("/how-far-back")}>How the Keele descent was broken →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Hanley to Sydney</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The 4th (King's Own)</h2>
    <p>Born at <strong>Hanley, Stoke-on-Trent, on 15 March 1810</strong> and christened there on
    28 April 1811, the son of <strong>Samuel Sneyd, a grocer of Hanley</strong>, and Elizabeth
    Margaret Oliver. In <strong>1832</strong> he came to New South Wales with the
    <strong>4th (King's Own) Regiment of Foot</strong> — one of the regiments doing convict-guard duty
    in the colony, the same work that had brought Major George Pitt D'Arcy out six years earlier.
    <a href={u("/australia")}>The other soldier in this family →</a></p>
    <p style="margin-top:.9rem">On <strong>13 February 1837</strong> he married
    <strong>Catherine Margaret Mulcahy</strong> at <strong>Camden</strong>. Their first child,
    <strong>William Hartley Sneyd</strong> — this archive's direct ancestor — was born at
    <strong>Kempsey</strong> the following year.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The army record</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Private 1463, 80th Foot, Mounted Police Attached</h2>

    <p>He is in the muster, and it complicates the obituary in an interesting way.</p>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.1rem">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Name</td><td><strong>Samuel Sneyd</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Year</td><td class="m"><strong>1841</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Rank</td><td>Private</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Service number</td><td class="m"><strong>1463</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Unit</td><td><strong>80th Foot</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sub unit</td><td><strong>Mounted Police Attached</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Location</td><td><strong>Parramatta, N.S.W.</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Reference</td><td class="mono">TNA WO 12/8483 · British Army, Worldwide Index 1841</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <strong>Two apparent contradictions, both of which resolve.</strong>
      <p style="margin-top:.7rem"><strong>The regiment.</strong> The obituary says the
      <em>4th (King's Own)</em>; the 1841 muster says the <strong>80th Foot</strong>. Both are likely
      true in sequence. The 4th left the colony in the later 1830s and the 80th replaced it — and a
      soldier who had just married locally, as he did at Camden in February 1837, would volunteer into
      the incoming regiment rather than sail home. The 80th were the
      <strong>Staffordshire Volunteers</strong>, which for a Hanley man is not a bad place to land.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:.7rem"><strong>The rank.</strong> The press calls him
      <em>Serjeant</em> Sneyd in 1839; the muster calls him a <em>private</em> in 1841. That is not a
      demotion. The New South Wales Mounted Police was staffed by detachments seconded from line
      regiments, and a man held police rank while remaining on his regiment's books at his regimental
      rank. He was a sergeant of police and a private of the 80th at the same time.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="small" style="margin-top:1.1rem"><strong>Checked and excluded.</strong> Several
    <em>Samuel Sneyd</em> entries in the Chelsea pension returns (WO 22) belong to a different man —
    54th Foot, pension paid at Dublin. They are not him, and are recorded here so that nobody attaches
    them later. <strong>Still wanted:</strong> the muster book WO 12/8483 itself, and any discharge
    papers. <a href={u("/open-questions")}>Queued →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">30 October 1839</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The taking of Chubb, Knowles and Rees</h2>

    <p>On <strong>29 October 1839</strong> two runaway convicts, <strong>Thomas Chubb</strong> and
    <strong>Frederick Knowles</strong>, together with a third man named <strong>Rees</strong>, robbed
    the house of a settler named Brown near the <strong>Vale of Clwydd</strong>, in the mountains west
    of Sydney.</p>

    <p style="margin-top:.9rem">The next day Brown rode for the police. What happened then was printed
    in Sydney nine days later.</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <span class="chip doc">the primary report</span> <strong>The Sydney Monitor and Commercial
      Advertiser</strong>, Friday <strong>8 November 1839</strong>, page 2, "News of the day".
      <span class="mono">nla.news-article32166178</span>
    </div>

    <blockquote style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      Mr. Brown proceeded to the mounted police station at <strong>Bowen's Hollow</strong>, when
      <strong>Serjeant Sneyd</strong> and a private trooper proceeded in quest of the bushrangers …
      <strong>Sneyd perceived them first</strong>; they were mounted and well armed; having
      <strong>crept through the bush until he came quite close to them, he issued from the bush and
      charged them</strong>. One of the bushrangers fired at the trooper but missed him … the serjeant
      fired after them and <strong>shot one of them dead</strong>, and after a little difficulty took
      the other two into custody.
    </blockquote>

    <p>The <em>Monitor</em>'s correspondent adds the detail the family story lost: the men had raided
    <strong>Mr Brown's mill on the Mudgee road</strong>, robbed the household and held the women at
    gunpoint; and when they were taken they had the stolen property on them and
    <strong>eight firearms</strong>. The paper commends Sneyd's bravery, and then turns to a complaint
    about lenient sentencing — which is how a piece of this family's history came to be printed at
    all.</p>

    <blockquote style="margin-top:1.1rem">
      Great praise is due to Serjeant Sneyd, who showed a great deal of courage in the taking of them.
    </blockquote>

    <p>Chubb and Knowles were tried at the <strong>Supreme Court of New South Wales on 1 February
    1840</strong> and convicted — reported as <em>R. v. Chubb</em> [1840] NSWSupC 4. The third man,
    Rees, was already dead.</p>

    <p style="margin-top:.9rem">Two later scholarly compilations of 1839–40 New South Wales court and
    press records give a fuller version of the pursuit: that Brown's neighbour Walker joined them, and
    with him <strong>two Aboriginal trackers</strong> — whose names none of the accounts record — who
    followed the bushrangers all day before the party came up with them near the head of Wallowa
    Creek.</p>

    <p class="small" style="margin-top:.9rem">The 1839 report and the later compilations differ on one
    point of choreography: the <em>Monitor</em> has Sneyd creeping up and charging mounted, armed men,
    while the secondary accounts have the party tracking all day and finding them encamped at night.
    Both mention the Aboriginal trackers. The newspaper is four days after the event and is preferred;
    the difference is recorded rather than smoothed away.</p>

    <p class="small" style="margin-top:1.1rem">The family tree's own note gives the place as
    "<em>Piper's Flat, about 25 miles from Bathurst</em>" and the third man as "Reis". Piper's Flat is
    in the same country, near Wallerawang; the spelling of the dead man's name varies between Rees and
    Reis across accounts. Neither difference is material, and both are recorded here rather than
    quietly harmonised.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">A career read off a family</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Where the children were born</h2>
    <p>He left no service file this archive has yet seen. But a policeman's postings can be read
    directly off the birthplaces of his children, and one of them is named for the town he was
    stationed in:</p>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1.1rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th class="m">Year</th><th>Place</th><th>What happened there</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {postings.map(([y, p, w]) => (
            <tr><td class="m">{y}</td><td><strong>{p}</strong></td><td>{w}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem"><strong>John Braidwood Sneyd</strong>, born at Braidwood in 1842, is
    the clearest of them — a child given the name of the place his father was posted. And
    <strong>Samuel Hartley Hanley Stafford Sneyd</strong>, born 1851, carries the family's Staffordshire
    origins in his own name: <em>Hanley</em>, the town, and <em>Stafford</em>, the county.</p>

    <p style="margin-top:.9rem">And his obituary supplies the rest of the career, which turns out to
    be a great deal more than "the Queensland police".</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">4 July 1885</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Governor of Brisbane Gaol</h2>

    <blockquote>
      Mr. Sneyd came to Australia in 1832 with the "4th of Kings" regiment. In the early days of New
      South Wales he was <strong>selected as one of the picked men from the regiment to suppress
      bushranging</strong> in that colony. While in the Mounted Police he rose to the position of
      <strong>sergeant-major of the Goulburn Division</strong>. He came to Moreton Bay as
      <strong>chief constable in 1849</strong>, and retired from that position in 1859, when he was
      promoted to fill the position of <strong>Governor of the gaol</strong>. Out of the six constables
      then in Brisbane two are now alive, namely, Mr. Samuel Wright and Mr. James Frederick.
      <footer class="small" style="font-style:normal;margin-top:.6rem">— <em>The Queenslander</em>,
      Saturday 11 July 1885, p. 67 · <span class="mono">nla.news-article19799164</span></footer>
    </blockquote>

    <p>He was <strong>chief constable at Moreton Bay from 1849</strong> — effectively the head of
    policing in Brisbane in the decade before Queensland separated from New South Wales — and then
    <strong>Governor of Brisbane Gaol from 1859</strong>. He retired to "a snug little farm" at
    <strong>Enoggera</strong>, drawing a New South Wales pension and a Queensland compensation
    payment.</p>

    <p style="margin-top:.9rem">He died there on the evening of <strong>Saturday 4 July 1885</strong>,
    aged 75. He had come into town on the Friday "in the best of spirits". He went to bed about ten,
    complained of feeling unwell ten minutes later, and was dead within fifteen minutes, before a
    doctor could reach him. Dr Hill's post-mortem gave the cause as <strong>heart disease</strong>.
    The obituary calls him "<em>a fine specimen of a bluff but kindly-hearted Englishman</em>".</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <strong>Where this account comes from, and why that matters.</strong> The biographical paragraph
      above is <em>not</em> the newspaper's own reporting. It is lifted almost word for word from a
      letter to the <em>Brisbane Courier</em> of <strong>7 July 1885</strong>, signed
      <strong>J. A. McIntyre</strong>, written to correct an earlier paragraph about the death.
      McIntyre's corrections are domestic and rather touching — Sneyd was <em>not</em> "enjoying a
      quiet pipe", because "he never indulged in the habit of smoking"; and he had <em>not</em> been
      shocked by reading of Mrs McLean's death, since he "conversed freely with his family on different
      topics for about three hours and a-half after reading the notice". So the career details are
      family testimony given four days after the death — excellent, but testimony, not a service
      record. <span class="mono">nla.news-article3443970</span>
    </div>

    <h3 style="margin-top:1.8rem">The gaol he ran</h3>
    <p>Two years into the job, <em>The Courier</em> sent a writer through the Brisbane Gaol and printed
    a long account of it. He was shown round by the governor:</p>

    <blockquote>
      by the kindness of <strong>Mr. Sneyd, the governor</strong>, we were made acquainted with the
      internal economy … the Brisbane Gaol was <strong>as well managed as it could be under the
      circumstances and the money voted for it</strong> … there is no overcrowding, the consequences of
      which are overcome only by <strong>the very best management on the part of the governor</strong>
      and those in authority.
      <footer class="small" style="font-style:normal;margin-top:.6rem">— <em>The Courier</em>
      (Brisbane), Saturday 22 August 1863, p. 3 · <span class="mono">nla.news-article3165233</span>.
      A second, longer account ran on 18 September 1863.</footer>
    </blockquote>

    <p>The writer notes that the prisoners' bread was "really good white, fresh, and wholesome", and
    that men leaving the gaol could take up employment — an unusually approving piece about a colonial
    prison, and the closest thing the archive has to a description of his working life.</p>

    <p style="margin-top:.9rem"><strong>And the gaol became a family trade.</strong> His son
    <strong>Joseph Sneyd</strong> was <em>chief warder at the Brisbane Gaol for thirty years</em> —
    recorded in the 1911 obituaries of Joseph's own son, published at Ipswich and Brisbane. Father
    governor, son chief warder, across half a century of the same building.</p>

    <p class="small" style="margin-top:1.4rem"><strong>Two discrepancies, recorded.</strong> The
    obituary places his death at <strong>Enoggera</strong>; the family tree says
    <strong>Westwood Lodge, Alderley</strong> — adjoining suburbs, and both may be right of the same
    holding. And it says his father died "at the advanced age of <strong>84</strong>", which for a
    death in 1855 gives a birth about 1771 — pulling against the Madeley baptism of 1781 and
    <em>towards</em> the tree's unsourced 1769. That one is genuinely open.
    <a href={u("/sneyd")}>The Madeley line →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>Two soldiers, one family</h2>
    <p class="dek">It is worth noticing what this archive is actually made of. Cristina's
    great-great-great-grandfather on one side was <strong>Major George Pitt D'Arcy of the 39th</strong>,
    who came out in charge of a convict guard in 1826. On the other, four generations back, was
    <strong>Sergeant-Major Samuel Charles Sneyd of the 4th King's Own</strong>, who came out on the same
    duty in 1832, stayed to police the colony, and ended as its gaoler. Neither family needed a castle. Both of them, when the
    invented ancestry is taken away, turn out to have been doing the actual work of the place.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><a href={u("/sneyd")}>The Sneyds →</a> ·
    <a href={u("/how-far-back")}>How far back it really goes →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
